**Theorem 21.2 (support radius and exact LAB one-Tick criterion).**
Let $s\to u$ be any finite raw unticked extension, including the empty
one, and let $z$ be strictly positive at every vertex of $V_s$.
For every integer $r\ge0$,

$$
\operatorname{supp}(T_u^r\iota z)
 =\{a\in V_u:\operatorname{dist}_u(a,V_s)\le r\}.
\tag{21.1}
$$

Consequently $T_u\iota z$ is positive on all $V_u$ if and only if
the extension edits each initial logical leaf cone at most once.
For a nonempty extension these are exactly the serial realizations
of Definition 21.1. Every permutation inside such a block is legal
and has the same literal term and field endpoint.

Proof. For $r=0$, support is precisely $V_s$. Multiplication by
$T_u=(4\operatorname{Id}+A_u)/8$ makes a coordinate positive precisely
when its old coordinate or an adjacent coordinate was positive:
all coefficients are nonnegative and the indicated diagonal and
edge coefficients are positive. There is no cancellation.
Thus support grows by exactly one closed graph neighborhood each
Tick, proving (21.1) inductively. This is the classical nonnegative
walk/support argument, used here on the actual persistent extension.

It remains to characterize distance at most one in LAB. The first
edit in an initial alpha cone is $P_p$, adding $pL$ adjacent to the
old $p$; the first in an initial beta cone is $S_p$, adding $pLL,pR$
adjacent respectively to the old $pL,p$. If each initial cone is
edited at most once, these are all the fresh cells, and all touch $V_s$.

Conversely consider the second edit within any one initial cone.
If its initial leaf was alpha, its first edit was $P_p$ and its
second must be $S_p$. The latter creates $pLL$, whose distance to
the old support is two. If its initial leaf was beta, its first
edit was $S_p$; the next edit in that cone is either $S_{pL}$,
creating $pLLL$, or $P_{pR}$, creating $pRL$. In either case the
displayed vertex has distance two to $V_s$. Intervening edits in
other initial cones do not change these paths. All future changes
attach vertices and edges to the persistent tree without deleting
old edges or adding a cycle; they cannot shorten this distance.
Therefore positivity after one Tick fails whenever a cone is edited
twice, proving the equivalence.

Different initial logical leaves are incomparable addresses.
A first edit in one cone changes no target constructor, old cell
or edge in another. Their fresh sets are disjoint, even when one
old leaf is a beta with its unary marker. Swapping adjacent edits
in distinct cones therefore preserves enabledness, final ordered
material and every field value: old fields are retained and only
disjoint new zeros are written. Any two enumerations of a finite
set are connected by such swaps. Hence every enumeration is legal
and has the same endpoint, as claimed. $\square$

